DSP.mod(16,{"m16-stats":{"id":"m16-stats","slug":"question-bank-statistics-and-probability","m":16,"title":"Question bank: statistics and probability","min":30,"type":"revise","priority":null,"prereq":[],"tags":["question-bank","statistics"],"qa":15,"ai":{"scope":"Rapid-fire statistics and probability questions with the course answers and deeper notes for revision.","keyTerms":["p-value","Type I and II error","CLT","t-test vs z-test","confidence interval","outliers","covariance","KS test","expected flips","Bayes","multicollinearity"],"interviewQs":["What is a p-value?","Expected number of coin flips for two consecutive heads?","Why might a test be significant but useless?"],"mistakes":["Giving definitions with no example","Mixing up confidence intervals and probabilities"],"practice":"Cover the answers, say each aloud in 30 seconds, reveal, and rate yourself honestly.","related":["m5-qa","m5-hypothesis","m5-puzzles"],"points":"Q: What is a p-value?\nA: Probability of data at least as extreme as observed, assuming H0 is true. Not P(H0 true).\n\nQ: Type I vs Type II error?\nA: False positive (alpha) vs false negative (beta). Power = 1 - beta.\n\nQ: Explain CLT.\nA: Sample means approach normal with SE = sigma/sqrt(n), regardless of population shape, for large n.\n\nQ: When to use t-test vs z-test?\nA: t when sigma unknown/small n; z when sigma known or n large (they converge).\n\nQ: Confidence interval meaning?\nA: Procedure that captures the true parameter in 95% of repeated samples.\n\nQ: How to handle outliers?\nA: Investigate; cap, transform, robust stats, or separate modeling.\n\nQ: Covariance vs correlation?\nA: Correlation = covariance scaled to [-1,1].\n\nQ: How to test if two distributions differ?\nA: KS test, Mann-Whitney, chi-square on binned data, permutation tests.\n\nQ: Why might a test be significant but useless?\nA: Tiny effect with large n; check effect size/CI and business impact.\n\nQ: Expected number of coin flips for two consecutive heads?\nA: 6.\n\nQ: Bayes on medical test?\nA: Use prior, sensitivity, false-positive rate; post-test probability may be low (see 5.2).\n\nQ: Multicollinearity effect?\nA: Unstable coefficients/inflated SEs, not reduced predictive power; use VIF/regularization."},"intuitive":"<p>This bank trains the thirty-second statistics answer. Every good answer has the same skeleton: one plain sentence that defines the idea, one tiny example with numbers, and one caution about how people misuse it. For instance, a p-value is how surprising your data would be if nothing were going on; example: p = 0.03 means a gap this big appears in about 3 of 100 no-effect experiments; caution: it is not the probability the effect is real. Say each answer aloud before revealing, then compare.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Statistics questions are short, and interviewers judge clarity and correctness within the first two sentences.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Define, example, caution, in under thirty seconds.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;For statistics questions I use a thirty-second formula. A plain definition first, then a small numeric example, then the common misunderstanding. A p-value: the probability of data at least this extreme if the null is true; with a conversion test, p of 0.03 means a gap like this would happen about three per cent of the time by chance; it is not the chance the null is true. Type one is a false alarm, type two a miss. The central limit theorem: averages become normal as n grows. Outliers: investigate before removing. Then I stop and let the interviewer follow up. The catch: answers without an example sound memorised.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-use-the-bank-well","text":"Use the bank well","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"16.1#1","src":"c","q":"What is a p-value?","a":"<p>Probability of data at least as extreme as observed, assuming H0 is true. Not P(H0 true).</p>\n","s":"The chance of seeing data at least this extreme if there were no real effect. It is not the probability that the null is true.","m":"<p><strong>Say it with numbers.</strong> In an A/B test of 10.0% versus 10.5% conversion, p = 0.03 says: if the true rates were equal, a gap this large would occur in about 3% of repeated experiments. Combine it with the confidence interval for the size of the lift.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.1#2","src":"c","q":"Type I vs Type II error?","a":"<p>False positive (alpha) vs false negative (beta). Power = 1 - beta.</p>\n","s":"Type I: false alarm, rejecting a true null (rate α). Type II: a miss, failing to reject a false null (rate β; power is 1 − β).","m":"<p><strong>Memory aid.</strong> Type I is the boy who cried wolf (false alarm); Type II is the wolf arriving unnoticed (a miss). Lowering α increases β unless you increase the sample size.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.1#3","src":"c","q":"Explain CLT.","a":"<p>Sample means approach normal with SE = sigma/sqrt(n), regardless of population shape, for large n.</p>\n","s":"Averages of many independent samples are roughly normal with SD σ/√n, whatever the original shape (finite variance).","m":"<p><strong>Why it matters in practice.</strong> CLT is why we can build confidence intervals and t-tests for means of skewed metrics like order value once the sample is large. It does not make individual values normal.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.1#4","src":"c","q":"When to use t-test vs z-test?","a":"<p>t when sigma unknown/small n; z when sigma known or n large (they converge).</p>\n","s":"z-test when population σ is known or n is large; t-test when σ is estimated from a small sample (heavier tails).","m":"<p><strong>In practice.</strong> With n above about 30 and unknown σ, t and z give nearly identical results; the t-test is the safe default because the difference vanishes as n grows.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.1#5","src":"c","q":"Confidence interval meaning?","a":"<p>Procedure that captures the true parameter in 95% of repeated samples.</p>\n","s":"A range from a procedure that covers the true value in 95% of repeats; not &quot;95% probability this interval holds it&quot;.","m":"<p><strong>Careful wording.</strong> &quot;If we repeated the sampling and interval procedure many times, about 95% of the intervals would contain the true value.&quot; Informally, people say &quot;we are 95% confident&quot;; avoid saying &quot;there is a 95% probability the true value is in this particular interval&quot; in a strict frequentist setting.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.1#6","src":"c","q":"How to handle outliers?","a":"<p>Investigate; cap, transform, robust stats, or separate modeling.</p>\n","s":"Investigate first: error, real extreme, or different population? Then fix, cap (winsorise), transform, or use robust methods.","m":"<p><strong>A decision path.</strong> Is it a typo or sensor fault? Fix or drop it. Is it real but extreme (a whale customer)? Keep it, use medians or a log transform, or report with and without. Never delete points only because they hurt the model.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.1#7","src":"c","q":"Covariance vs correlation?","a":"<p>Correlation = covariance scaled to [-1,1].</p>\n","s":"Covariance shows direction but depends on units; correlation divides by both SDs to give −1 to 1 and measures linear association.","m":"<p><strong>Example.</strong> Height in cm and weight in kg have a covariance of 60 (hard to interpret); changing to metres shrinks it by 100. The correlation of 0.7 is unit-free.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.1#8","src":"c","q":"How to test if two distributions differ?","a":"<p>KS test, Mann-Whitney, chi-square on binned data, permutation tests.</p>\n","s":"Continuous: KS test or compare means with a t-test; categorical: chi-square; always plot both distributions too.","m":"<p><strong>Choose by data type.</strong> Two continuous samples: two-sample KS test (any difference in shape) or a t-test for the mean only. Categorical counts: chi-square. Always add a histogram or ECDF plot.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.1#9","src":"c","q":"Why might a test be significant but useless?","a":"<p>Tiny effect with large n; check effect size/CI and business impact.</p>\n","s":"Huge samples make tiny effects significant; significance does not show size or business value.","m":"<p><strong>Example.</strong> With 10 million users, a 0.01% lift is statistically significant yet worth less than the engineering cost; compare the interval to the minimum effect worth shipping.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.1#10","src":"c","q":"Expected number of coin flips for two consecutive heads?","a":"<ol start=\"6\">\n<li></li>\n</ol>\n","s":"6, from solving E = 1 + ½H + ½E and H = 1 + ½E.","m":"<p><strong>Derivation.</strong> Let E be the expected flips from scratch and H the expected flips remaining after a first head. E = 1 + ½H + ½E and H = 1 + ½E (with probability ½ the next flip is heads and you finish). Solving gives E = 6. (For HT it is 4.)</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.1#11","src":"c","q":"Bayes on medical test?","a":"<p>Use prior, sensitivity, false-positive rate; post-test probability may be low (see <a href=\"#/s/5.2\">5.2</a>).</p>\n","s":"With 1% prevalence, 99% sensitivity and 5% false positives, a positive means about 16.7% chance of disease: base rates matter.","m":"<p><strong>Numbers.</strong> With 1% prevalence, 99% sensitivity, 5% false-positive rate: P(D | +) = 0.0099 / 0.0594 = 16.7%. With 10% prevalence it would be 68.8%. See <a href=\"#/s/5.2\">Bayes&#39; theorem</a>.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.1#12","src":"c","q":"Multicollinearity effect?","a":"<p>Unstable coefficients/inflated SEs, not reduced predictive power; use VIF/regularization.</p>\n","s":"Correlated predictors make coefficients unstable with huge standard errors, though predictions can remain fine; check VIF.","m":"<p><strong>Detect and fix.</strong> Variance inflation factor above about 5 to 10 is a warning. Drop or combine correlated features, use PCA, or ridge regression. Predictions are fine; interpretation of individual coefficients is not.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-use-the-bank-well\">Use the bank well</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Cover the answer</strong> and answer aloud in under 30 seconds.</li>\n<li><strong>Reveal</strong>, compare, and rate: Got it, Shaky or Missed.</li>\n<li>For each Shaky or Missed, open the linked topic, then <strong>re-answer a day later</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<details class=\"more\"><summary>How to get the most from this bank</summary><div class=\"more-b\"><p>Work in three passes. <strong>Pass 1:</strong> answer aloud with the timer, reveal, rate yourself. <strong>Pass 2:</strong> for every Shaky or Missed item, open the linked topic and rewrite the answer in your own words. <strong>Pass 3:</strong> two days later, answer the weak ones again. Items you can answer with a number-based example are safe; items where you only recite a definition are not yet ready.</p>\n</div></details>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-stats~ca0_1","q":"Give the example and the caution for &quot;confidence interval&quot;.","a":"<p>Example: a 95% CI of 10% ± 1% for conversion from 10,000 users. Caution: it does not mean there is a 95% probability that the truth lies in this particular interval.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-stats~ca0_2","q":"Why can a result be significant but useless?","a":"<p>With huge samples a tiny, practically worthless difference becomes significant; always report effect size and its business value.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-stats~ca0_3","q":"Expected flips to see two heads in a row: the answer and the idea?","a":"<p>Six flips. Set up expected values with states (no head yet, one head so far) and solve the two linear equations.</p>\n"}]},"m16-ml-1":{"id":"m16-ml-1","slug":"question-bank-machine-learning-i","m":16,"title":"Question bank: machine learning I","min":30,"type":"revise","priority":null,"prereq":[],"tags":["question-bank","machine-learning"],"qa":15,"ai":{"scope":"Machine learning revision questions 1 to 12 with complete answers where the course points to another section.","keyTerms":["bias-variance","bagging vs boosting","L1 sparsity","XGBoost vs GBM","data leakage","accuracy on imbalanced data","precision recall","ROC vs PR","k-means","decision tree splits","tree scaling","overfitting in neural nets"],"interviewQs":["Bagging vs boosting?","What is data leakage?","Why do tree models not need scaling?"],"mistakes":["Answering \"see the notes\" instead of having the answer ready","Forgetting trade-offs"],"practice":"Answer each question in under 60 seconds including one example.","related":["m7-qa","m7-gradient-boosting","m7-leakage"],"points":"Q: Bias-variance tradeoff?\nA: Simpler models high bias/low variance; complex reverse; balance for minimum generalization error.\n\nQ: Bagging vs boosting?\nA: \n\nQ: Why L1 sparse, L2 not?\nA: Diamond vs circle constraint; constant vs proportional gradient.\n\nQ: How does XGBoost differ from GBM?\nA: Regularization, second-order gradient, sparsity-aware splits, parallelism, handling missing values.\n\nQ: What is data leakage?\nA: \n\nQ: Accuracy on 99:1 data?\nA: A trivial \"always negative\" model scores 99%; use PR-AUC/recall/precision/F1, class weights, threshold tuning.\n\nQ: Precision vs recall tradeoff?\nA: Threshold slides along the PR curve; choose by cost.\n\nQ: ROC-AUC vs PR-AUC?\nA: ROC can be misleading under heavy imbalance since TN dominates FPR; PR focuses on positive class.\n\nQ: How to choose K in K-Means?\nA: Elbow, silhouette, business constraints.\n\nQ: How does a decision tree choose splits?\nA: Maximizes impurity reduction (Gini/entropy/variance).\n\nQ: Why do tree models not need scaling?\nA: Splits depend on order, not magnitude.\n\nQ: How to reduce overfitting in neural nets?\nA: Dropout, weight decay, early stopping, augmentation, smaller model."},"intuitive":"<p>These twelve questions are the classic machine learning screen. Behind them sit just a few ideas: models can be too simple or too flexible (bias and variance); there are two ways to combine models (bagging and boosting); penalties shrink weights (L1 and L2); scores can lie (accuracy on imbalanced data, leakage); and thresholds trade precision against recall. A strong answer gives the idea, the reason, an example and the trade-off, in about a minute.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers use these questions to see whether your fundamentals are solid before going deeper.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Idea, reason, example, trade-off.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;For the classic ML questions I answer in four beats: the idea, the reason, an example and the trade-off. Bias-variance: simple models underfit, flexible ones overfit; example depth two versus depth thirty trees; control with regularisation and validation. Bagging versus boosting: bagging averages independent models to cut variance; boosting corrects errors in sequence to cut bias. Accuracy on ninety-nine to one data is meaningless, since always predicting the majority scores ninety-nine; use precision, recall and PR-AUC. Trees do not need scaling because they only compare against thresholds. The catch: trade-offs show maturity, so I never leave an answer without one.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#1","src":"c","q":"Bias-variance tradeoff?","a":"<p>Simpler models high bias/low variance; complex reverse; balance for minimum generalization error.</p>\n","s":"Error = bias² + variance + noise; simple models underfit, flexible models overfit; tune complexity with validation.","m":"<p><strong>Picture it.</strong> Fit a straight line to curved data: wrong in a consistent way (bias). Fit a 15-degree polynomial to ten points: perfect on those points, wild on new ones (variance). The best model sits in between; validation error shows where.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#2","src":"c","q":"Bagging vs boosting?","a":"<p><strong>Bagging</strong> trains many independent models in parallel on bootstrap samples and averages them, mainly reducing <strong>variance</strong> (random forest: deep trees plus random feature subsets). <strong>Boosting</strong> trains models sequentially, each one fitting the errors (residuals or gradients) of the ensemble so far, mainly reducing <strong>bias</strong> (shallow trees, learning rate). Bagging is robust and hard to overfit; boosting is usually more accurate when tuned but needs early stopping and is more sensitive to noise. Stacking is a third option: a meta-model learns to combine different base models. See <a href=\"#/s/7.3.5\">gradient boosting</a> for details.</p>\n","s":"Bagging: parallel independent models averaged to cut variance. Boosting: sequential models each fixing prior errors to cut bias.","m":"<p><strong>Why each works.</strong> Averaging models that make different errors cancels noise, so bagging helps unstable, deep trees. Boosting uses weak learners (shallow trees) and adds capacity step by step, so it lowers bias but can overfit noise; use early stopping.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#3","src":"c","q":"Why L1 sparse, L2 not?","a":"<p>Diamond vs circle constraint; constant vs proportional gradient.</p>\n","s":"L1&#39;s constant pull and diamond-shaped constraint zero out weights; L2&#39;s pull fades as weights shrink, so they get small, not zero.","m":"<p><strong>Geometry.</strong> Minimising loss within |w1| + |w2| ≤ t: the diamond has corners on the axes, and the loss contours usually touch a corner, where one weight is zero. With w1² + w2² ≤ t, the circle has no corners.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#4","src":"c","q":"How does XGBoost differ from GBM?","a":"<p>Regularization, second-order gradient, sparsity-aware splits, parallelism, handling missing values.</p>\n","s":"XGBoost adds regularisation, second-order gradients, sparsity handling, parallel and cache-aware speed on top of classic GBM.","m":"<p><strong>Practical differences.</strong> XGBoost is regularised (L1/L2 on leaf weights), handles missing values natively, and is much faster; LightGBM and CatBoost are further variants for speed and categorical data.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#5","src":"c","q":"What is data leakage?","a":"<p><strong>Data leakage</strong> is information from outside the training context leaking into the model, so offline scores are inflated and production performance disappoints. Examples: scaling or imputing on the whole dataset before splitting; target encoding computed on the same rows; features containing future information (refunds after purchase); random splits for time series or repeated customers; duplicates across train and test; a proxy for the label. Signal: suspiciously high AUC or one dominant feature. Prevention: split first, use a <code>Pipeline</code>, time- or group-aware splits, and audit each feature for availability at prediction time. See <a href=\"#/s/7.2\">leakage</a>.</p>\n","s":"Using information at training time that will not exist at prediction time, such as target-derived features or preprocessing fit on all data.","m":"<p><strong>Checklist.</strong> Does every feature exist at prediction time? Was each transformer fit on training data only? Are duplicates or the same customer split across train and test? Is the split by time when forecasting?</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#6","src":"c","q":"Accuracy on 99:1 data?","a":"<p>A trivial &quot;always negative&quot; model scores 99%; use PR-AUC/recall/precision/F1, class weights, threshold tuning.</p>\n","s":"Predicting always-majority gives 99% accuracy and finds no positives; use precision, recall, PR-AUC and weights.","m":"<p><strong>Numbers.</strong> With 1% positives, &quot;always negative&quot; gets 99% accuracy and 0% recall. Use PR-AUC, recall and precision at a business-driven threshold, <code>class_weight</code>, and stratified splits.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#7","src":"c","q":"Precision vs recall tradeoff?","a":"<p>Threshold slides along the PR curve; choose by cost.</p>\n","s":"Raising the threshold increases precision but lowers recall; choose the point by the relative cost of false positives and negatives.","m":"<p><strong>Numbers.</strong> Threshold 0.5 gives precision 80%, recall 50%. Lowering to 0.3 may give precision 60%, recall 75%. Choose where expected cost (cost_FN × FN + cost_FP × FP) is lowest.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#8","src":"c","q":"ROC-AUC vs PR-AUC?","a":"<p>ROC can be misleading under heavy imbalance since TN dominates FPR; PR focuses on positive class.</p>\n","s":"ROC-AUC measures ranking over all thresholds but looks optimistic with rare positives; PR-AUC focuses on the positive class.","m":"<p><strong>Why ROC misleads.</strong> FPR = FP / (FP + TN); with a huge number of negatives the TN term is enormous, so even many false positives barely move FPR and the ROC curve looks excellent. Precision = TP / (TP + FP) uses FP directly, so PR curves expose the problem. On a problem with 2% positives the same scores gave ROC-AUC 0.86 but average precision 0.20.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#9","src":"c","q":"How to choose K in K-Means?","a":"<p>Elbow, silhouette, business constraints.</p>\n","s":"Elbow of inertia, silhouette score, stability, and whether the segments are actionable.","m":"<p><strong>Procedure.</strong> Run K-means for k = 2…10, plot inertia (elbow) and silhouette. Prefer the k where silhouette is high and the segments make business sense and are stable across seeds.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#10","src":"c","q":"How does a decision tree choose splits?","a":"<p>Maximizes impurity reduction (Gini/entropy/variance).</p>\n","s":"It tries candidate splits and picks the one with the largest impurity reduction (Gini, entropy, or variance), recursively.","m":"<p><strong>Example.</strong> A node with 50 spam and 50 ham has Gini 0.5. A split sending 45 spam and 5 ham left, 5 spam and 45 ham right gives each child Gini 0.18, so the gain is 0.32; the tree picks the split with the largest gain.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#11","src":"c","q":"Why do tree models not need scaling?","a":"<p>Splits depend on order, not magnitude.</p>\n","s":"Splits compare one feature to a threshold, so monotonic rescaling does not change the tree.","m":"<p><strong>Why.</strong> A split like &quot;income &gt; 40,000&quot; depends only on the ordering of income values. Any monotonic rescaling (standardising, log) leaves the ordering, and therefore every possible split, unchanged.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#12","src":"c","q":"How to reduce overfitting in neural nets?","a":"<p>Dropout, weight decay, early stopping, augmentation, smaller model.</p>\n","s":"Dropout, weight decay, early stopping, data augmentation, more data, or a smaller network.","m":"<p><strong>Priority order.</strong> More or augmented data first, then dropout and weight decay, early stopping on validation loss, then reduce capacity.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<details class=\"more\"><summary>Turn each answer into a four-beat reply</summary><div class=\"more-b\"><p>For every question in this bank write four short lines: <strong>Idea</strong> (one plain sentence), <strong>Reason</strong> (the mechanism), <strong>Example</strong> (a number or a tiny scenario), <strong>Trade-off</strong> (what it costs or when it fails). Example for &quot;bagging versus boosting&quot;: Idea, bagging averages independent models while boosting adds models in sequence. Reason, averaging cuts variance, sequential correction cuts bias. Example, random forest versus XGBoost on tabular data. Trade-off, boosting overfits noisy labels without early stopping. Do this for the questions you rate Shaky and say them aloud.</p>\n</div></details>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-ml-1~ca0_1","q":"Answer &quot;bias-variance trade-off&quot; in four beats.","a":"<p>Idea: error splits into bias (too simple) and variance (too sensitive). Reason: flexibility lowers one and raises the other. Example: depth-2 versus depth-30 tree. Trade-off: tune complexity with validation and regularise.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-ml-1~ca0_2","q":"Why is accuracy misleading at 99:1 and what do you report?","a":"<p>Predicting always-majority scores 99%. Report precision, recall, PR-AUC and a cost-based threshold.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-ml-1~ca0_3","q":"Why does L1 give sparse weights and L2 does not?","a":"<p>L1&#39;s constant-size pull (and diamond-shaped constraint) can push weights exactly to zero; L2&#39;s pull shrinks with the weight, so weights get small but not zero.</p>\n"}]},"m16-ml-2":{"id":"m16-ml-2","slug":"question-bank-machine-learning-ii","m":16,"title":"Question bank: machine learning II","min":30,"type":"revise","priority":null,"prereq":[],"tags":["question-bank","machine-learning"],"qa":16,"ai":{"scope":"Machine learning revision questions 13 to 25 with extended notes.","keyTerms":["cross-validation","regularisation and lambda","gradient descent","high-cardinality categoricals","calibration","SHAP","PCA vs feature selection","RF importance bias","logistic regression to a business person","small data","missing values in production","concept drift"],"interviewQs":["When is vanilla k-fold cross-validation wrong?","How do you explain model predictions?","How would you detect concept drift?"],"mistakes":["Forgetting time or group structure in cross-validation","Treating importance as causation"],"practice":"Answer each in under 60 seconds and add one example from your projects.","related":["m7-qa","m8-classification-metrics","m12-key-concepts"],"points":"Q: What is cross-validation and when not to use vanilla k-fold?\nA: Rotating validation folds; not for time series or grouped data.\n\nQ: What is regularization and how to choose lambda?\nA: Penalty on complexity; choose via CV.\n\nQ: Explain gradient descent and learning rate.\nA: Iteratively move against gradient; LR too high diverges, too low is slow.\n\nQ: How do you handle categorical variables with 1000+ levels?\nA: Target encoding (out-of-fold), frequency encoding, hashing, embeddings, CatBoost.\n\nQ: What is calibration?\nA: Predicted probabilities match observed frequencies; check reliability curve; fix with Platt/isotonic.\n\nQ: How to explain model predictions?\nA: SHAP (global and local), permutation importance, PDP, LIME.\n\nQ: Model performs well offline, poorly online?\nA: \n\nQ: Difference between PCA and feature selection?\nA: PCA creates new combined features; selection keeps a subset of originals.\n\nQ: How is Random Forest feature importance biased?\nA: Impurity importance favors high-cardinality/continuous features; use permutation importance.\n\nQ: Explain logistic regression to a business person.\nA: It estimates the probability of an event from a weighted combination of factors; each factor raises or lowers the odds.\n\nQ: What if training data is small?\nA: Simpler/regularized models, CV, augmentation, transfer learning, domain priors, collect more.\n\nQ: Handling missing values in production?\nA: Same imputation as training, missing flags, monitoring for new missingness patterns.\n\nQ: How would you detect concept drift?\nA: Monitor performance, PSI on inputs/outputs, statistical tests, retrain triggers."},"intuitive":"<p>These thirteen questions go one level deeper than the classics: validation schemes, regularisation strength, categorical features with many levels, probability calibration, explaining predictions, and what to do when the offline score does not survive production. The common thread is practical judgement. For each, ask yourself: what would I actually do, what could go wrong, and how would I check? That is the answer.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> These questions separate people who have only read about ML from those who have shipped it.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Answer with what you would do, the risk, and the check.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;These questions are about judgement, so I answer with action, risk and check. Cross-validation estimates generalisation by rotating validation folds; I avoid plain k-fold for time series and grouped data, where I use forward-chaining and group splits. I choose the regularisation strength by validation. For a categorical column with a thousand levels I use target or frequency encoding done out-of-fold, hashing, or embeddings. If a model works offline but not online, I check leakage, training-serving skew, drift and metric mismatch. For small data I use simple models, strong regularisation and cross-validation. The catch: every answer should state how I would verify it.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#13","src":"c","q":"What is cross-validation and when not to use vanilla k-fold?","a":"<p>Rotating validation folds; not for time series or grouped data.</p>\n","s":"Rotate validation folds to estimate generalisation; do not use vanilla k-fold for time series (use forward chaining) or grouped data (use group k-fold).","m":"<p><strong>Example.</strong> For a churn model with multiple rows per customer, plain k-fold puts the same customer in train and validation folds, leaking customer-specific patterns: use <code>GroupKFold</code>. For monthly forecasting, use <code>TimeSeriesSplit</code> so you always validate on the future.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#14","src":"c","q":"What is regularization and how to choose lambda?","a":"<p>Penalty on complexity; choose via CV.</p>\n","s":"A penalty on weight size to reduce overfitting; choose λ by cross-validation on a grid or random search.","m":"<p><strong>Choosing λ.</strong> Search on a log scale (for example 1e-4 to 1e2) using cross-validation. Pick the value with the best validation score, or the largest λ within one standard error of the best for a simpler model.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#15","src":"c","q":"Explain gradient descent and learning rate.","a":"<p>Iteratively move against gradient; LR too high diverges, too low is slow.</p>\n","s":"Step the weights against the gradient of the loss; the learning rate sets step size: too big diverges, too small is slow.","m":"<p><strong>Intuition.</strong> Imagine walking downhill in fog: the gradient is the slope under your feet, and the learning rate is your stride. Too long a stride and you overshoot the valley; too short and you take forever.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#16","src":"c","q":"How do you handle categorical variables with 1000+ levels?","a":"<p>Target encoding (out-of-fold), frequency encoding, hashing, embeddings, CatBoost.</p>\n","s":"Frequency or target encoding computed out-of-fold, hashing, grouping rare levels, or embeddings; avoid one-hot.","m":"<p><strong>Code sketch.</strong> Out-of-fold smoothed target encoding: for each fold compute category means on the other folds, blend with the global mean using a pseudo-count, and apply to the held-out fold. CatBoost implements an ordered version natively.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#17","src":"c","q":"What is calibration?","a":"<p>Predicted probabilities match observed frequencies; check reliability curve; fix with Platt/isotonic.</p>\n","s":"Whether predicted probabilities match observed frequencies; check with a reliability curve and fix with Platt scaling or isotonic regression.","m":"<p><strong>Check.</strong> Bin predicted probabilities (for example deciles) and compare with the observed positive rate per bin: a calibrated model lies on the diagonal. Platt scaling fits a logistic regression on the scores; isotonic regression fits a monotone step function (needs more data).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#18","src":"c","q":"How to explain model predictions?","a":"<p>SHAP (global and local), permutation importance, PDP, LIME.</p>\n","s":"Global: feature importance and partial dependence. Local: SHAP or LIME for a single prediction. Or use an interpretable model.","m":"<p><strong>Pick by audience.</strong> Executives: top three drivers in plain words. Analysts: SHAP summary plot. Customers (for a decision such as a loan): reason codes for that single prediction.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#19","src":"c","q":"Model performs well offline, poorly online?","a":"<p><strong>A model that scores well offline but poorly online</strong> usually points to one of: <strong>leakage</strong> in offline evaluation; <strong>train-serve skew</strong> (features computed differently in production: units, joins, time windows, missing handling); a <strong>distribution mismatch</strong> between the offline test set and live traffic (time, geography, user mix); <strong>drift</strong> since training; <strong>feature unavailability or latency</strong> at inference forcing simpler inputs; <strong>feedback loops</strong>; or an overfit validation set after heavy tuning. Debug by logging production features and comparing them with offline features for the same entities. See <a href=\"#/s/12.3\">MLOps Q&amp;A</a>.</p>\n","s":"Check leakage, training-serving skew, drift, a mismatched metric, and feedback effects, then compare feature distributions.","m":"<p><strong>Debug order.</strong> First compare feature values between offline and online for the same entity (skew). Then check label definitions and the time window. Then compare population distributions, then the metric alignment.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#20","src":"c","q":"Difference between PCA and feature selection?","a":"<p>PCA creates new combined features; selection keeps a subset of originals.</p>\n","s":"PCA builds new combined features (unsupervised, loses interpretability); feature selection keeps a subset of the originals.","m":"<p><strong>Example.</strong> PCA turns 100 correlated features into 10 components that are blends of all 100; feature selection keeps, say, 12 of the original columns, so you can still say &quot;income&quot; matters.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#21","src":"c","q":"How is Random Forest feature importance biased?","a":"<p>Impurity importance favors high-cardinality/continuous features; use permutation importance.</p>\n","s":"Impurity-based importance favours high-cardinality and continuous features and splits credit among correlated ones; use permutation importance or SHAP.","m":"<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use permutation importance on a validation set (shuffle one feature and measure the score drop) or SHAP values, which are less biased towards high-cardinality features.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#22","src":"c","q":"Explain logistic regression to a business person.","a":"<p>It estimates the probability of an event from a weighted combination of factors; each factor raises or lowers the odds.</p>\n","s":"&quot;It gives the probability of the outcome; each factor raises or lowers the odds by a fixed multiple, so we can say how much each matters.&quot;","m":"<p><strong>Plain-language version.</strong> &quot;It gives each customer a score for how likely they are to do something, say cancel. Each factor, like late deliveries or months as a customer, pushes the score up or down by a fixed amount, and we add them up. So we can tell you exactly why a customer has a high score.&quot;</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#23","src":"c","q":"What if training data is small?","a":"<p>Simpler/regularized models, CV, augmentation, transfer learning, domain priors, collect more.</p>\n","s":"Prefer simple, regularised models, use cross-validation, transfer learning or augmentation, and consider more labelling.","m":"<p><strong>Playbook.</strong> Strong regularisation, simpler model, repeated cross-validation, pretrained features or embeddings, data augmentation, and a plan to collect more labels where the model is most uncertain.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#24","src":"c","q":"Handling missing values in production?","a":"<p>Same imputation as training, missing flags, monitoring for new missingness patterns.</p>\n","s":"Use the same imputation as training with fitted values, add missing flags, alert on missing-rate changes, and have a fallback.","m":"<p><strong>Production recipe.</strong> Save imputation values with the model, add a was-missing flag, log the missing rate per feature, alert on jumps, and choose a safe default behaviour when a critical feature is missing.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.2#25","src":"c","q":"How would you detect concept drift?","a":"<p>Monitor performance, PSI on inputs/outputs, statistical tests, retrain triggers.</p>\n","s":"Monitor input and prediction distributions (PSI, KS), performance on delayed labels and proxy metrics; investigate when shifts or drops appear.","m":"<p><strong>Practical monitoring.</strong> Track rolling performance once labels arrive, PSI on inputs and on the score, the positive rate of predictions, and calibration drift. Concept drift often shows first as calibration or rank-performance decay on recent labelled data, while input drift can be present without hurting performance.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<details class=\"more\"><summary>Judgement questions: the action, risk, check pattern</summary><div class=\"more-b\"><p>These questions ask what you would do. Answer with <strong>Action</strong> (the concrete step), <strong>Risk</strong> (what could go wrong) and <strong>Check</strong> (how you would know). For &quot;model works offline, fails online&quot;: Action, compare offline and online feature values for the same entities. Risk, training-serving skew or leakage. Check, log served features and replay them through the offline pipeline. Practise each of the thirteen this way.</p>\n</div></details>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-ml-2~ca0_1","q":"A model&#39;s offline AUC is 0.92 but online conversion shows no gain. Name three causes.","a":"<p>Leakage or skew between offline and online features; the metric does not match the business goal (AUC versus revenue); distribution drift or feedback effects in production.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-ml-2~ca0_2","q":"Why not use plain k-fold on user-level data with many rows per user?","a":"<p>Rows from the same user fall in both train and validation, leaking identity; use group k-fold by user.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-ml-2~ca0_3","q":"What does calibration mean and when does it matter?","a":"<p>Predicted probabilities match observed frequencies (of cases scored 0.7, about 70% are positive); it matters when probabilities drive decisions, such as pricing or risk.</p>\n"}]},"m16-sql-python":{"id":"m16-sql-python","slug":"question-bank-sql-and-python-rapid-fire","m":16,"title":"Question bank: SQL and Python rapid-fire","min":20,"type":"revise","priority":null,"prereq":[],"tags":["question-bank","sql","python"],"qa":13,"ai":{"scope":"Rapid-fire SQL and Python/pandas questions with runnable answers where the course gives a one-liner.","keyTerms":["second highest salary","consecutive months","UNION vs JOIN","percent change by category","swap variables","Counter","generator vs list comprehension","dropna","rank within groups","chained assignment"],"interviewQs":["Second highest salary without window functions?","Find users active in consecutive months.","Why does df[df.a > 1]['b'] = 0 fail?"],"mistakes":["Leaving out NULL and ties handling","Forgetting sort before a window function"],"practice":"Write each solution in a scratch file before revealing it, then run it on the sample data.","related":["m2-level-2","m3-pandas-checklist","m1-checklist"],"points":"Q: Second highest salary without window functions?\nA: SELECT MAX(salary) FROM e WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM e);\n\nQ: Find users active in consecutive months?\nA: Use LAG on month or gaps-and-islands.\n\nQ: Difference between UNION and JOIN?\nA: UNION stacks rows; JOIN combines columns based on keys.\n\nQ: How to find the percent change between two periods per category?\nA: Window LAG per category or self-join.\n\nQ: Python: swap two variables without a temp?\nA: a, b = b, a.\n\nQ: Python: count word frequency in text?\nA: Counter(text.lower().split()).\n\nQ: Python: what's the output of [i for i in range(3)] vs (i for i in range(3))?\nA: List [0, 1, 2] vs generator object.\n\nQ: pandas: drop rows where any/all values NaN?\nA: dropna(how='any'/'all').\n\nQ: pandas: rank within groups?\nA: groupby(...)['col'].rank(method='dense', ascending=False).\n\nQ: pandas: why df[df.a > 1]['b'] = 0 fails?\nA: Chained assignment on a copy; use df.loc[df.a > 1, 'b'] = 0."},"intuitive":"<p>These are the rapid-fire coding questions: ten that interviewers use to test fluency, not cleverness. For SQL: second-highest without window functions, consecutive-month activity, UNION versus JOIN, percentage change. For Python and pandas: swapping variables, counting words, list versus generator, NaN handling, ranking within groups, and the copy-versus-view trap. Practise writing each from a blank page in under three minutes, then compare with the answer.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Fluency on small problems frees your attention for the hard ones.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Write it blind, run it mentally on a tiny input, then check.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Rapid-fire coding questions test fluency. For the second highest salary without windows, I take the maximum salary below the overall maximum, or order descending and offset one with a distinct. Users active in consecutive months: truncate to month, then join the table to itself on the next month, or use LAG. UNION stacks result rows vertically, JOIN combines columns horizontally by a key. In Python, a list comprehension builds the whole list in memory and a generator expression computes lazily, one item at a time. In pandas, dropna with how equal to any or all, groupby rank, and the chained-assignment trap where I should use loc. The catch: I test each on a tiny input by hand.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"16.3#1","src":"c","q":"Second highest salary without window functions?","a":"<p><code>SELECT MAX(salary) FROM e WHERE salary &lt; (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM e);</code></p>\n","s":"<code>SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp WHERE salary &lt; (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp)</code>; or <code>ORDER BY salary DESC</code> with <code>LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1</code> on DISTINCT salaries.","m":"<p><strong>Edge cases.</strong> With ties at the top (two people at the maximum), this returns the next <em>distinct</em> salary, which is usually what &quot;second highest&quot; means. If there is only one distinct salary it returns NULL. With a window function: <code>SELECT DISTINCT salary FROM (SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) r FROM e) t WHERE r = 2</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.3#2","src":"c","q":"Find users active in consecutive months?","a":"<p>Use LAG on month or gaps-and-islands.</p>\n","s":"Reduce to distinct user-month, then self-join on next month or use LAG and check the gap is one month.","m":"<p><strong>SQL.</strong> Users with consecutive months of activity:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> m <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> user_id, DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> mth <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DISTINCT</span> a.user_id\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> m a <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">JOIN</span> m b <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ON</span> a.user_id <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> b.user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> b.mth <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> a.mth <span class=\"hljs-operator\">+</span> <span class=\"hljs-type\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;1 month&#x27;</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<p>For longer streaks, number the months and use the gaps-and-islands subtraction trick.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.3#3","src":"c","q":"Difference between <code>UNION</code> and <code>JOIN</code>?","a":"<p>UNION stacks rows; JOIN combines columns based on keys.</p>\n","s":"UNION stacks rows from two queries with the same columns; JOIN combines columns from tables by matching keys.","m":"<p><strong>Example.</strong> <code>SELECT a FROM t1 UNION SELECT a FROM t2</code> stacks rows (same columns); <code>SELECT * FROM t1 JOIN t2 ON t1.id = t2.id</code> adds columns from matching rows. UNION changes the number of rows, JOIN changes the number of columns.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.3#4","src":"c","q":"How to find the percent change between two periods per category?","a":"<p>Window LAG per category or self-join.</p>\n","s":"(current − previous) / NULLIF(previous, 0), using LAG partitioned by category and ordered by period.","m":"<p><strong>SQL.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WITH</span> m <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> category, DATE_TRUNC(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;month&#x27;</span>, order_date) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> mth, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> rev <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> sales <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> category, mth, rev,\n       <span class=\"hljs-number\">100.0</span> <span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span> (rev <span class=\"hljs-operator\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(rev) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> category <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> mth)) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">/</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">NULLIF</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">LAG</span>(rev) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> category <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> mth), <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> pct_change\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> m;</code></pre></div>\n<p>The <code>PARTITION BY category</code> makes each category compare only with its own previous month.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.3#5","src":"c","q":"Python: swap two variables without a temp?","a":"<p><code>a, b = b, a</code>.</p>\n","s":"<code>a, b = b, a</code> (tuple unpacking).","m":"<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The right side builds a tuple (b, a) first, then it is unpacked into a and b, so no temporary variable is needed.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.3#6","src":"c","q":"Python: count word frequency in text?","a":"<p><code>Counter(text.lower().split())</code>.</p>\n","s":"<code>collections.Counter(text.split())</code>, or a dict with <code>d[w] = d.get(w, 0) + 1</code>.","m":"<p><strong>Code.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> collections <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> Counter\nCounter(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;the cat and the hat&quot;</span>.split()).most_common(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>)    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [(&#x27;the&#x27;, 2), (&#x27;cat&#x27;, 1)]</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Normalise first (<code>lower()</code>, strip punctuation) if the question needs it.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.3#7","src":"c","q":"Python: what&#39;s the output of <code>[i for i in range(3)]</code> vs <code>(i for i in range(3))</code>?","a":"<p>List <code>[0, 1, 2]</code> vs generator object.</p>\n","s":"The first is the list [0, 1, 2]; the second is a lazy generator object.","m":"<p><strong>Memory.</strong> <code>[i for i in range(3)]</code> builds the list <code>[0, 1, 2]</code> immediately. <code>(i for i in range(3))</code> prints as <code>&lt;generator object &lt;genexpr&gt; at 0x...&gt;</code> and produces values one at a time; wrap it in <code>list()</code> to see them, and note it can be consumed only once.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.3#8","src":"c","q":"pandas: drop rows where any/all values NaN?","a":"<p><code>dropna(how=&#39;any&#39;/&#39;all&#39;)</code>.</p>\n","s":"<code>df.dropna()</code> drops rows with any NaN; <code>df.dropna(how=&#39;all&#39;)</code> drops rows where all values are NaN.","m":"<p><strong>Options.</strong> <code>dropna(subset=[&#39;col&#39;])</code> considers only some columns; <code>thresh=k</code> keeps rows with at least k non-null values; <code>axis=1</code> drops columns.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.3#9","src":"c","q":"pandas: rank within groups?","a":"<p><code>groupby(...)[&#39;col&#39;].rank(method=&#39;dense&#39;, ascending=False)</code>.</p>\n","s":"<code>df.groupby(&#39;g&#39;)[&#39;x&#39;].rank(method=&#39;dense&#39;, ascending=False)</code>.","m":"<p><strong>Example.</strong> <code>df[&quot;rank&quot;] = df.groupby(&quot;dept&quot;)[&quot;salary&quot;].rank(method=&quot;dense&quot;, ascending=False)</code> (method <code>&quot;min&quot;</code> is RANK, <code>&quot;first&quot;</code> is ROW_NUMBER).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.3#10","src":"c","q":"pandas: why <code>df[df.a &gt; 1][&#39;b&#39;] = 0</code> fails?","a":"<p>Chained assignment on a copy; use <code>df.loc[df.a &gt; 1, &#39;b&#39;] = 0</code>.</p>\n","s":"Chained indexing assigns to a copy; use <code>df.loc[df.a &gt; 1, &#39;b&#39;] = 0</code>.","m":"<p><strong>Fix.</strong> <code>df.loc[df.a &gt; 1, &quot;b&quot;] = 0</code>: a single <code>.loc</code> call with the row mask and column performs the assignment on the original frame. The chained form assigns into a temporary object.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<details class=\"more\"><summary>A routine for blind coding practice</summary><div class=\"more-b\"><p>Cover the answer. Write the solution on paper or a plain editor with a three-minute timer. Run it by hand on a three-row input. Then compare with the answer and note any difference: a missing edge case (NULL, ties, empty input), a slower approach, or a syntax slip. Re-solve the ones you missed after two days. Speed on these small problems is built by repetition, not by reading.</p>\n</div></details>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-sql-python~ca0_1","q":"Second highest salary without window functions?","a":"<p><code>SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp WHERE salary &lt; (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp)</code>; returns NULL if there is no second value.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-sql-python~ca0_2","q":"<code>[i for i in range(3)]</code> versus <code>(i for i in range(3))</code>?","a":"<p>The first is the list [0, 1, 2]; the second is a generator object that yields values lazily and can be consumed once.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-sql-python~ca0_3","q":"<code>df[df.a &gt; 1][&#39;b&#39;] = 0</code> does not change df. Why, and the fix?","a":"<p>It assigns to a temporary copy from the chained indexing; use <code>df.loc[df.a &gt; 1, &#39;b&#39;] = 0</code>.</p>\n"}]},"m16-case":{"id":"m16-case","slug":"question-bank-case-and-product","m":16,"title":"Question bank: case and product","min":25,"type":"revise","priority":null,"prereq":[],"tags":["question-bank","case-study"],"qa":10,"ai":{"scope":"Case and product revision questions with model answers that expand the course's pointers into complete outlines.","keyTerms":["metric drop","feature success","A/B test design","churn model","fraud detection","recommendations","data bug"],"interviewQs":["Orders dropped 10%. Investigate.","How would you detect fraud at a payments company?","You discover a data bug affecting last month's reports. What do you do?"],"mistakes":["Unstructured answers","Skipping the decision and the action"],"practice":"Answer each in three minutes aloud using the frameworks, then reveal and compare.","related":["m13-metric-drop","m13-feature-success","m6-lifecycle","m13-problem-framing"],"points":"Q: Orders dropped 10%. Investigate.\nA: \n\nQ: Measure success of a new feature.\nA: \n\nQ: Design an A/B test for checkout redesign.\nA: \n\nQ: Build a churn model: walk through target definition, features, split, metrics, deployment, action.\nA: \n\nQ: How would you detect fraud at a payments company?\nA: Rules + supervised model + anomaly detection, velocity/device/graph features, recall at fixed precision, feedback loop from disputes, latency constraints.\n\nQ: How would you build a recommendation for a food delivery app?\nA: Retrieval (popularity, CF, embeddings) + ranking (GBM/NN with context like time, location, weather) + diversity/business rules; metrics: CTR, conversion, GMV, repeat rate; A/B test.\n\nQ: You discover a data bug affecting last month's reports. What do you do?\nA: Quantify impact, inform stakeholders quickly, fix and backfill, add tests/monitoring, document postmortem."},"intuitive":"<p>Case questions have no single right answer, so the interviewer is watching your structure. A good case answer follows a repeatable path: clarify the goal, pick the metrics, break the problem into parts, say what data you need, state the risks, and finish with a recommendation. The seven cases here (metric drop, feature success, A/B test design, churn, fraud, recommendations, data bug) reuse the same few frameworks, so learn the frameworks once and apply them seven times.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> The case round is where hiring managers decide whether you think like a data scientist.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Clarify, metric, structure, data, risks, recommendation.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;For any case I use one skeleton. Clarify the goal and the decision. Define the metric and guardrails. Break the problem into parts. Say what data I need. Name the risks and biases. Finish with a recommendation and a next step. For a ten per cent drop in orders: verify the data, scope the timing, decompose orders into visitors times conversion times basket, segment by platform and region, check releases and external factors. For a churn model: define churn and the prediction window, build features as of the prediction date, split by time, choose the metric from costs, and plan deployment and monitoring. The catch: finish with a clear recommendation, not a list of ideas.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"16.4#1","src":"c","q":"Orders dropped 10%. Investigate.","a":"<p><strong>Structure (see <a href=\"#/s/13.2\">diagnosing a metric drop</a>):</strong> (1) Verify the drop is real (pipeline, logging, definitions, dashboard lag). (2) Decompose: orders = users × sessions per user × conversion × orders per converter; find which factor moved. (3) Segment by platform, app version, city, new vs returning, channel, category. (4) Internal causes: releases, pricing, promotions ending, supply issues, experiments. (5) External causes: festivals, weather, competitors, events, outages. (6) Funnel: where did drop-off rise? (7) Prioritise hypotheses by evidence and impact, recommend action and monitoring. Example: traffic steady, conversion down only on Android 4.2, payment success fell from 94% to 81%: a payment-SDK bug, so hotfix or roll back.</p>\n","s":"Verify the data, scope the timing, decompose into traffic × conversion × basket, segment, check releases and external causes, then act.","m":"<p><strong>Example decomposition.</strong> Orders = sessions × conversion × orders per converter. If sessions are flat and conversion fell only on Android after release 4.2, the investigation is narrow: check the app version funnel and crash logs.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.4#2","src":"c","q":"Measure success of a new feature.","a":"<p><strong>Structure (see <a href=\"#/s/13.3\">measuring feature success</a>):</strong> goal and target users; one primary metric linked to the north star, secondary diagnostics, guardrails; leading vs lagging indicators; an A/B test (or a quasi-experiment if randomisation is impossible); segments and cannibalisation; decision criteria set in advance (ship, iterate, kill). Always address what the feature might steal from elsewhere.</p>\n","s":"State the goal; choose a primary metric, adoption and guardrails; use an A/B test or a rollout comparison; set ship/iterate/kill rules beforehand.","m":"<p><strong>Example.</strong> For a reorder button: primary metric is repeat orders per user in 14 days; adoption is taps per eligible user; guardrails are order value and support contacts; decide with an A/B test.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.4#3","src":"c","q":"Design an A/B test for checkout redesign.","a":"<p><strong>Structure (see <a href=\"#/s/6.1\">the experiment lifecycle</a>):</strong> hypothesis (&quot;a shorter form will raise checkout conversion&quot;); primary metric checkout conversion, secondary time to complete and payment errors, guardrails latency, refunds and support contacts; randomise by user; sample size from baseline and MDE (for 10% to 11%, about 14,700 per group); run at least one or two weekly cycles; check SRM; analyse per user with a confidence interval; decision rule written in advance. Mention peeking, novelty, and segment analysis as hypothesis generation.</p>\n","s":"Hypothesis, primary metric (checkout conversion) and guardrails, randomise by user, compute sample size and duration, run whole weeks, pre-plan analysis and decision.","m":"<p><strong>Design.</strong> Baseline conversion 3%, detect +0.3pp: n ≈ 16 × 0.029 / 0.003² ≈ 51,000 per group. At 10,000 eligible users a day split in two, that is about 10 days; run two full weeks.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.4#4","src":"c","q":"Build a churn model: walk through target definition, features, split, metrics, deployment, action.","a":"<p><strong>Structure (see <a href=\"#/s/13.4\">ML problem framing</a>):</strong> decision (who gets a retention offer) and cost of errors; target (no order in the next 30 days from a snapshot date); features as of the snapshot (recency, frequency and trend, service quality, support, engagement); time-based split and baseline then gradient boosting; metrics PR-AUC, lift at the contact budget and expected profit; threshold from costs; deployment as weekly batch scoring with monitoring (PSI) and a holdout to measure real retention impact (consider uplift modelling).</p>\n","s":"Define churn and window, features as of prediction date, time-based split, cost-based metric, threshold, then deploy, monitor and link to an action.","m":"<p><strong>Details.</strong> Churn = no order in the next 30 days among customers active in the last 90. Features use only data before the snapshot date. Evaluate precision at the top decile of risk because the retention budget is limited.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.4#5","src":"c","q":"How would you detect fraud at a payments company?","a":"<p>Rules + supervised model + anomaly detection, velocity/device/graph features, recall at fixed precision, feedback loop from disputes, latency constraints.</p>\n","s":"Rules plus a model on velocity, device, geography and amount features; handle imbalance and delayed labels; metric at a review budget; real-time scoring and feedback loop.","m":"<p><strong>Fuller outline.</strong> <em>Data and labels:</em> transaction, device, geography and customer history; labels arrive late (chargebacks) and are biased toward cases that were flagged. <em>Features:</em> velocity counts per card/device/IP, amount relative to the customer&#39;s norm, new device or location, merchant risk, graph features (shared devices). <em>Models:</em> rules for known patterns, a supervised model (gradient boosting) for recall, an anomaly model for novelty. <em>Metric:</em> recall at a fixed false-positive rate, PR-AUC, precision@review-capacity. <em>Serving:</em> real-time under tens of milliseconds with online features. <em>Loop:</em> analyst feedback and disputes become labels; monitor drift and adversarial adaptation.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.4#6","src":"c","q":"How would you build a recommendation for a food delivery app?","a":"<p>Retrieval (popularity, CF, embeddings) + ranking (GBM/NN with context like time, location, weather) + diversity/business rules; metrics: CTR, conversion, GMV, repeat rate; A/B test.</p>\n","s":"Two stages: candidate retrieval (collaborative and content signals, context such as time and location), then a ranker; handle cold start; evaluate offline then A/B on orders and retention.","m":"<p><strong>Fuller outline.</strong> Retrieval (popularity, collaborative filtering, cuisine and location filters, open restaurants only), then a ranking model on user, restaurant and context features predicting order probability, then re-ranking for diversity and new-restaurant exploration. Cold start through popularity and content; offline NDCG with a time split; online A/B test on conversion, GMV and repeat rate, with delivery-time guardrails.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.4#7","src":"c","q":"You discover a data bug affecting last month&#39;s reports. What do you do?","a":"<p>Quantify impact, inform stakeholders quickly, fix and backfill, add tests/monitoring, document postmortem.</p>\n","s":"Size the impact, tell stakeholders promptly, fix and backfill, add data-quality checks, and write up the root cause.","m":"<p><strong>Fuller outline.</strong> Quantify the impact first (which reports, which decisions, how large); notify stakeholders promptly and plainly; fix the root cause and <strong>backfill</strong> corrected data; version and annotate the corrected reports; add tests and monitoring (row-count and freshness checks, reconciliation with source systems) so it cannot recur silently; write a short postmortem (what happened, impact, detection, fix, prevention). Transparency and speed matter more than perfection.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<details class=\"more\"><summary>One skeleton for every case</summary><div class=\"more-b\"><p>Clarify the goal and decision; choose the metric and guardrails; break the problem into parts (decompose, segment, or stage); list the data you need; name risks and biases; end with a recommendation and next step. Apply it to each of the seven cases above and write each answer on one page. If a case does not fit the skeleton, ask which part you skipped.</p>\n</div></details>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-case~ca0_1","q":"You discover a data bug affecting last month&#39;s reports. What do you do?","a":"<p>Quantify the impact and scope, tell stakeholders immediately and plainly, fix the pipeline and backfill corrected numbers, add checks and tests to prevent recurrence, and document what happened.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-case~ca0_2","q":"Walk through the skeleton for &quot;detect fraud at a payments company&quot;.","a":"<p>Goal and cost of errors; labels (chargebacks arrive late); features such as velocity, device, amount versus history; time-based split; metric precision/recall at a review budget; real-time scoring with rules plus model; monitoring and feedback loop.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-case~ca0_3","q":"Why should a case answer end with a recommendation?","a":"<p>It shows you can turn analysis into a decision; a list of ideas leaves the interviewer to do that work.</p>\n"}]},"m16-self-test":{"id":"m16-self-test","slug":"self-test-answer-aloud-in-under-two-minutes","m":16,"title":"Self-test: answer aloud in under two minutes","min":30,"type":"practice","priority":null,"prereq":[],"tags":["question-bank","practice"],"qa":8,"ai":{"scope":"Five self-test questions to answer aloud in under two minutes each, with model answer outlines.","keyTerms":["explain gradient boosting","walk through your best project","logistic regression vs XGBoost","check whether a feature is worth shipping","correlation vs causation"],"interviewQs":["Explain how gradient boosting works to a beginner.","Why would you choose logistic regression over XGBoost?","How would you check whether a new feature is worth shipping?"],"mistakes":["Going over two minutes","Using jargon with a non-technical audience"],"practice":"Record yourself answering each question in under two minutes, listen back, and trim filler words.","related":["m16-ml-1","m14-deep-dive","m6-lifecycle"],"points":"Q: Explain how gradient boosting works to a beginner.\nA: \n\nQ: Walk me through your best project as if I am a hiring manager.\nA: \n\nQ: Why would you choose logistic regression over XGBoost?\nA: \n\nQ: How would you check whether a new feature is worth shipping?\nA: \n\nQ: What is the difference between correlation and causation, with an example from your domain?\nA: "},"intuitive":"<p>This is your final check: five questions you must be able to answer aloud in under two minutes, without notes. They test explanation (gradient boosting to a beginner), storytelling (your best project), judgement (logistic regression versus XGBoost), product sense (is the feature worth shipping) and clear thinking (correlation versus causation). Record yourself, play it back, and listen for jargon, missing examples and rambling. If you can do all five calmly, you are close to ready.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Being able to explain simply is the strongest sign that you understand.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Record, listen back, cut the jargon, repeat.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;The self-test is about explaining under time pressure. I aim for under two minutes, structured: a one-sentence answer, the reason, one example, one caveat. Gradient boosting to a beginner: many small decision trees, each one trained to fix the mistakes of the ones before, like a team where each new member focuses on what the others got wrong. Logistic regression over XGBoost when I need interpretability, a stable baseline, small data or a regulatory explanation. Correlation versus causation: ice cream sales and drownings rise together because of summer; only randomisation or careful design shows cause. The catch: I record myself, because rambling is invisible from the inside.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-how-to-use-this-page","text":"How to use this page","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"16.5#1","src":"c","q":"Explain how gradient boosting works to a beginner.","a":"<p>&quot;Imagine guessing house prices. Start with the average price for everyone. That guess is wrong by different amounts for different houses. Now train a tiny decision tree whose only job is to predict <em>those errors</em>: maybe &#39;big houses in this area are underestimated by ₹20 lakh&#39;. Add a fraction of its correction to the guess. Some errors remain, so train another tiny tree on the remaining errors, and add a fraction again. After hundreds of small corrections, the guess is very accurate. The &#39;fraction&#39; is the learning rate, and small steps stop us from overfitting. The &#39;gradient&#39; in the name means we correct the errors in the direction that most reduces the loss. Caution: because each tree depends on the previous one, we need early stopping on a validation set, or it will start fitting noise.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Many small trees built in sequence, each trained to correct the mistakes of the ones before, and added up with a small step size.","m":"<p><strong>A beginner script.</strong> &quot;Imagine predicting house prices. First guess the average. Look at where you were wrong. Train a small decision tree to predict those errors. Add a fraction of it to your guess. Repeat a hundred times. Each tree fixes a little of what remains.&quot;</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.5#2","src":"c","q":"Walk me through your best project as if I am a hiring manager.","a":"<p>Use this outline (90 to 120 seconds): <strong>Problem and decision</strong> (who uses it, what changes) → <strong>Data</strong> (source, size, grain, key issues) → <strong>Approach</strong> (split strategy, baseline, features, models tried and why) → <strong>Results</strong> (metric vs baseline, what the number means in business terms) → <strong>Insights and failure modes</strong> (error analysis, explainability) → <strong>Production thinking</strong> (how you would deploy and monitor) → <strong>What I would do next.</strong> Start with the headline: &quot;I built X that does Y, improving Z from A to B,&quot; then go through the layers, inviting the interviewer to dig into any part.</p>\n","s":"Problem and impact, your role, data and approach, the result with a number, what you learned: about two minutes, outcome first.","m":"<p><strong>Structure.</strong> 20 seconds on the problem and why it mattered, 20 on the data, 40 on the approach and a key decision, 20 on the result with a number, 20 on what you would improve.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.5#3","src":"c","q":"Why would you choose logistic regression over XGBoost?","a":"<p>&quot;I would choose logistic regression when <strong>interpretability, stability and regulation</strong> matter, when data is small or mostly linear, when I need well-calibrated probabilities, or when I want a robust baseline and easy monitoring: each coefficient gives an odds ratio I can explain to a risk or compliance team. XGBoost usually wins on raw accuracy with non-linearities and interactions and many features, but it is harder to explain and to monitor, and needs tuning and early stopping. In practice I start with logistic regression as the baseline; if XGBoost&#39;s lift is meaningful for the business (measured on a time-based validation set, at the operating threshold) and I can explain it with SHAP, I use it, otherwise I ship the simpler model.&quot;</p>\n","s":"When I need interpretability, a fast stable baseline, well-calibrated probabilities, small data, or regulatory explanations.","m":"<p><strong>Add the caveat.</strong> If the data has many non-linear interactions and accuracy matters most, XGBoost may be better; show you would test both, then explain the trade-off with SHAP.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.5#4","src":"c","q":"How would you check whether a new feature is worth shipping?","a":"<p>&quot;I would run an A/B test. Define the hypothesis, one primary metric linked to the goal, secondary metrics and guardrails (latency, errors, refunds). Randomise by user, compute the sample size from the baseline and minimum detectable effect, run for whole weekly cycles, check for sample ratio mismatch, and analyse per user with a confidence interval. Decide using effect size and cost, not just significance, and look at segments as hypotheses. If an experiment is not possible, use a quasi-experiment such as difference-in-differences and be upfront about the weaker evidence.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Define the goal, run an A/B test on the primary metric with guardrails, and pre-agree the effect size that justifies shipping.","m":"<p><strong>Sketch.</strong> Primary metric and guardrails, test unit, sample size, duration, pre-set decision rule, and who will act on the result.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"16.5#5","src":"c","q":"What is the difference between correlation and causation, with an example from your domain?","a":"<p>&quot;Correlation means two things move together; causation means changing one <em>causes</em> the other to change. Example from delivery: orders with more items have longer delivery times, but adding items does not make the rider slower: the confounder is <strong>order size and restaurant prep time</strong>. Ice-cream sales and drownings correlate because of summer. To establish causation we randomise (an A/B test) so groups differ only by the treatment, or use careful causal methods such as difference-in-differences when we cannot. Correlation is still useful for prediction, but dangerous for decisions about what to change.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Correlation is co-movement; causation means changing one changes the other. Ice cream and drownings both rise in summer; only experiments or good designs show cause.","m":"<p><strong>Own-domain example.</strong> In e-commerce, users who see more recommendations buy more, but heavy buyers also browse more, so the correlation overstates the effect. A randomised holdout reveals the true lift.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-how-to-use-this-page\">How to use this page</h3>\n<p>Try all five aloud first, then open each model answer and compare structure (not words). Note where you used jargon without explanation.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-self-test~ca0_1","q":"Give a 20-second beginner explanation of gradient boosting.","a":"<p>Start with a rough guess, measure the mistakes, train a small tree to predict those mistakes, add it with a small weight, and repeat many times until the errors are small.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-self-test~ca0_2","q":"Three reasons to choose logistic regression over XGBoost?","a":"<p>Interpretability (coefficients and odds ratios), a strong, fast baseline with little tuning, and regulatory or small-data settings; its probabilities are also usually well calibrated.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m16-self-test~ca0_3","q":"Give a domain example of correlation without causation and the way to test causation.","a":"<p>Ads shown more to high-intent users correlate with sales; randomise exposure in an A/B test (or use an instrument or holdout) to measure the true effect.</p>\n"}]}});